# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FraudLens
#
# Every rule here maps to something this stack actually produces: Python 3.11 +
# uv (ruff, mypy, pytest, coverage), React + Vite + TypeScript (Vitest, ESLint,
# Prettier, Tailwind), Terraform, Vercel, Supabase, Docker/Kubernetes, and the
# Claude Code / Codex agent tooling.
#
# The upstream GitHub Python template's sections for tools this repo does not
# use were removed deliberately — Django, Flask, Scrapy, Celery, Redis, RabbitMQ,
# ActiveMQ, PyInstaller, Sphinx, mkdocs, poetry/pdm/pipenv/pixi, tox/nox,
# nose/hypothesis, Jupyter/IPython, Spyder, Rope, Pyre, pytype, Cython, SageMath,
# Marimo, Streamlit, Abstra. A rule for a tool nobody runs is dead weight that
# obscures which artifacts are real.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Python bytecode and build output (uv build / hatchling)
__pycache__/
*.py[codz]
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# Virtualenv — `uv sync --all-packages` writes .venv/
.venv/

# Test, coverage, and analysis caches (pytest, coverage.py, ruff, mypy)
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.dmypy.json

# Logs are never source. Also covers Terraform's crash.log / crash.*.log.
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environments & local data (FraudLens)
#   Secrets come from Infisical at runtime, never from a file (Golden Rule 3).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vite loads .env.local and .env.[mode](.local) at build time. frontend/.env.production
# is tracked because the Vercel build reads it from the checkout; .env.example documents
# the non-secret local keys.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.development
!.env.example
!frontend/.env.production

# direnv: .envrc can carry exported credentials, so it stays ignored even though the
# repo does not require direnv. (Its .direnv/ cache is not ignored — nothing creates one.)
.envrc

# FraudLens local-demo state: docker volumes are named, but local-FS artifacts,
# the local ChromaDB index, and any scratch output live under .local/ (never committed).
.local/

# Real AML training datasets: raw CSVs are download/cache-only and NEVER committed
# (license + PHI posture). The fetch script writes under settings.aml_data_dir (default
# .local/aml_data, already covered above); /data/aml_data is belt-and-suspenders in case
# the dir is repointed. Kaggle API-token files are ignored anywhere in the tree.
/.local/aml_data/
/data/aml_data/
**/kaggle.json

# Model artifacts: the demo model_artifacts_dir is data/models. TWO bundles are tracked by
# exact-path negation below — the local-demo fixture (v0-fixture) and the story's pinned
# bundle. Everything else here is a locally trained candidate (from `make train-model`) and
# must NOT be committed.
/data/models/*
!/data/models/v0-fixture/
# The portfolio demo story pins this bundle by version label and calibrates every expected
# band, alert and SAR state against it, so it is a fixed artifact rather than a candidate: the
# bootstrap refuses any other model. `COPY data /app/data` bakes it into the image and the
# deploy runner reads it from the checkout, so tracking it is what makes the demo deployable.
!/data/models/xgb-ibm-aml-fs2-9d43c5f92a/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent tooling & secrets (FraudLens)
#   Shared agent config stays tracked: .claude/settings.json, .claude/skills/,
#   AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md. Only machine-specific and sensitive material is ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Claude Code per-developer overrides and worktrees. The worktree rule lived only in
# .git/info/exclude, which is machine-local, so every other clone saw it as untracked.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/

# Codex project-local state (global config lives in ~/.codex)
.codex/

# code-review-graph MCP graph cache (repo-local; rebuilt on demand)
.code-review-graph/

# Skill bundles are redundant once unpacked into .claude/skills/
*-skill.tar.gz

# Secret material — credentials come from Infisical, never the repo
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets/
.akeyless/
akeyless-token*
credentials.json
service-account*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node / frontend
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
frontend/dist/
coverage/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform — state is never committed; provider lock IS (see keep-tracked below)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.tfstate*
.terraform/
*.tfplan

# Variable files: local and ad-hoc tfvars — including *.auto.tfvars, which Terraform loads
# without being asked — are ignored, because that is where credentials land by accident. The
# four committed env inputs are non-secret by construction (account ids and operator values
# arrive as TF_VAR_* from the OIDC pipeline) and the guarded Make targets read them from the
# checkout, so each is negated by exact path rather than by a pattern that could widen silently.
*.tfvars
!infra/terraform/environments/aks-demo/aks-demo.tfvars
!infra/terraform/environments/data-batch/data-batch.tfvars
!infra/terraform/environments/dev/dev.tfvars
!infra/terraform/environments/prod/prod.tfvars

# Machine-local Terraform CLI config (.terraformrc / terraform.rc carry provider mirrors and
# credentials blocks) and override files, which merge over the committed config unannounced.
.terraformrc
terraform.rc
override.tf
*_override.tf

# Generated from the committed backend.tf.template (by deploy-backend.yml in CI, or by
# hand locally). Ignored so the template stays the single source of truth (rule 5).
infra/terraform/environments/*/backend.tf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vercel/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors & OS
#   Editor state is the most common accidental commit in a public repo, and git
#   itself writes .orig/.rej on a failed merge or patch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.code-workspace
.DS_Store
._*
*.orig
*.rej

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw screen captures
#   The committed deliverables are the exported GIF/MP4 under docs/demo/. The
#   source recordings they are cut from run to hundreds of MB and must never
#   reach history, where they cannot be removed without a rewrite.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.mov

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep-tracked (Golden: reproducible builds). Negations document intent even
# though nothing above currently ignores these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!uv.lock
!frontend/package-lock.json
!**/.terraform.lock.hcl
